'''Vegetarianism and veganism in Balisca''' refer to the practice and cultural institution of [[W:Plant-based diet|plant-based diets]] within the [[Baliscan Confederation|Baliscan]] population. Balisca has one of the highest rates of [[W:Vegetarianism|vegetarianism]] in the world, with an estimated 50 to 59 percent of Baliscans identifying as vegetarian or [[W:Veganism|vegan]] — of whom approximately 10 to 12 percent of the total population identify specifically as vegan.

The prevalence of plant-based eating is deeply rooted in the country's indigenous Kurupiranga and Wallche dietary traditions, reinforced by the influence of [[W:Buddhism and vegetarianism|Buddhist]] and [[W:Vegetarianism in Hinduism|Hindu vegetarian]] heritage, the Catharist ascetic tradition of [[Cerdanya]], and the ecological-political movements of the modern confederation. Vegetarianism in Balisca is not merely a dietary preference but a cultural institution, supported by the [[Green Protocol]], the [[Low Impact Initiative]], and the broader framework of Baliscan eco-socialist and regenerative economic policy.

The high rate of vegetarianism is frequently cited as a contributing factor to Balisca's low rate of adult obesity — among the lowest in the [[Abyalan Mutual Development Community]] (AMDC) — and to the confederation's high [[Human Development Index|HDI]] score. 

== History ==

=== Indigenous roots ===
{{See also|Anhangá curse}}
The deepest roots of Baliscan vegetarianism lie in the dietary practices of the [[Kurupiranga]] and [[Wallche]] peoples, for whom plant-based eating was not an ideological choice but an ecological reality. The indigenous communities of the archipelago maintained extensive knowledge of edible plants, forest mushrooms, [[W:Tubers|tubers]], and [[W:Fruits|fruits]], and their subsistence patterns were predominantly plant-based — meat was consumed, but as a supplement rather than a staple, and primarily during ceremonial or seasonal occasions. 

The Kurupiranga concept of ''ñuú-ka'' — roughly translatable as "eating with the earth" or "eating what the earth gives freely" — described a dietary ethic of taking only what the land provided without requiring the raising and killing of animals. This concept was not an absolute prohibition on meat, but a strong preference for plant-based sustenance that shaped indigenous food culture for centuries before colonial contact.

The Wallche communities of the [[Matarrañya]] highlands practiced a similar tradition, rooted in the mountain environment's limited capacity for [[W:Livestock|livestock]]. The highland diet was overwhelmingly composed of potatoes, quinoa, wild greens, and the abundant herbs of the alpine meadows, with meat (typically guanaco-equivalent or goat) reserved for ceremonial feasts. The Wallche concept of ''küme mogen'' — "living well" or "living in balance" — encompassed dietary moderation and a preference for plant foods as part of a broader ethic of ecological harmony.

=== Colonial and post-colonial period ===

The arrival of Mesogean colonists and enslaved Kaftians in the 16th and 17th centuries introduced meat-heavy dietary traditions — the Volisanian emphasis on [[W:Pork|pork]] and [[W:Beef|beef]], the Bayani and Imfezzan traditions of consuming lamb and goat, the South and West Kaftian use of smoked fish — that displaced indigenous plant-based practices in many coastal and lowland communities. However, the indigenous dietary tradition never disappeared, particularly in the interior highlands and the Maroon communities of [[Araucaria]], where the Kurupiranga and Wallche foodways were maintained alongside the new ingredients and techniques introduced by immigrant communities.

The [[Catharism|Catharist]] communities of [[Cerdanya]] and [[Estuaria]] introduced the most ideologically committed vegetarian tradition; Cathar doctrine rejected the raising and killing of animals for food as a fundamental violation of spiritual principle, and thus the Cathar diet was overwhelmingly vegetarian as a result — not as a periodic observance but as a consistent, daily practice. The Cathar culinary tradition — known as ''[[komida de kasta]]'' (pure food) — centered on simple, herb-rich preparations of vegetables, legumes, bread, mountain greens, and cheese, and it created a deeply rooted culture of plant-based eating in the highland communities that long outlasted its theological origins. Modern Cerdanyan veganism draws directly on the Cathar heritage, extending the rejection of animal products from meat to dairy and eggs.

The arrival of [[W:Buddhism|Buddhism]] to the [[Buddhism in Balisca|Northern Triangle]], first in the colonial era ([[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese exiles]]), and primarily in the 19th and early 20th centuries, brought the most robust vegetarian tradition to the country. Buddhist vegetarian practices — rooted in subcontinental religious and philosophical traditions — were adapted to Baliscan ingredients, producing the distinctive Buddhist Baliscan cuisine that remains the foundation of vegetarian cooking in [[Estuaria]]. The Buddhist immigrant community established many of the first dedicated vegetarian restaurants and food cooperatives in the country, and their culinary influence extended well beyond their own community during the [[Special Period]] with the expansion of [[People's commune|communal kitchens]].

=== Revolutionary and modern period ===

The [[Baliscan Revolution|revolutionary period]] after 1922 brought a new dimension to Baliscan vegetarianism: the ideological. The early confederal government's emphasis on communal self-sufficiency and regenerative agriculture created institutional support for plant-based eating, not as a moral imperative but as a practical one — plant-based agriculture was more efficient, more sustainable, and more compatible with the communal land management system being established across the confederation. The [[Plan de Vivienda Popular|''Plan de Vivienda Popular'']] (1922–29) included provisions for communal vegetable gardens and legume cultivation as part of the broader project of food sovereignty.

The environmental movement of the 1960s and 1970s, culminating in the election of [[Otávia Brito]] ([[Ecological Action]], Secretary General 1977–1983), transformed vegetarianism from a cultural practice into a political one. The [[Green Protocol]], first adopted during the Brito administration, explicitly linked dietary choices to ecological impact and established the concept of ''komida di terra'' — "food of the earth" — as a framework for plant-forward eating aligned with regenerative principles. The Green Protocol did not mandate vegetarianism, but it created institutional incentives: plant-based meals were prioritized in communal dining halls, agricultural subsidies favored legume and vegetable production over livestock, and public health campaigns emphasized the ecological and health benefits of reduced meat consumption.

The [[Low Impact Initiative]] and the subsequent [[Casajuan Plan]] further embedded plant-based eating into Baliscan institutional life. The Casajuan Plan's requirement that all residential conversions include communal kitchen facilities — designed around plant-based cooking — reflected the assumption that komida di tera was the default dietary practice in Baliscan urban communities.

== Prevalence ==
As of the 2026 confederal census, approximately 50 to 59 percent of Baliscans identify as vegetarian or vegan, with vegans constituting roughly 10 to 12 percent of the total population (approximately 20 percent of the vegetarian-and-vegan population). These rates vary significantly by community, age, and urbanization:

* [[Estuaria]] has the highest rate of vegetarianism and veganism in the confederation, at approximately 65 percent, reflecting the strong influence of early Buddhist and Hindu influence, alongside the region's agricultural character.
* [[Cerdanya]] reports rates of approximately 58 percent, attributed to traditional Catharist dietary tradition and the highland diet's historical emphasis on plant foods.
* [[Araucaria]] reports rates of approximately 52 percent, reflecting the indigenous and Maroon dietary traditions and [[South Kaftia|South Kaftian]] legume-based cuisine.
* [[Hesperia]] and the [[Castejón]] metropolitan area report the lowest rates, at approximately 38–42 percent, reflecting the greater diversity of dietary traditions and the influence of Mesogean meat-heavy cuisine in urban centers.
* [[Bayara]] reports approximately 45 percent, with the cattle-ranching interior showing lower rates than the [[Salsipuedes Sea]] coast.

Vegetarianism and veganism are most prevalent among Baliscans under 35, with rates exceeding 60 percent in the 18–34 age cohort, suggesting that the practice is becoming more rather than less widespread over time. The [[Education in Balisca|confederal education system's]] inclusion of ecological dietary education — a requirement under the Green Protocol — is frequently cited as a factor in the high rate of vegetarianism among younger Baliscans.

== Cultural and political dimensions ==

=== ''Komida de terra'' ===

The concept of ''komida de terra'' (KDT) — "food of the earth" — is the central organizing principle of Baliscan plant-based eating. Unlike the vegetarianism of many other cultures, which is often framed in terms of animal welfare or individual health, komida di tera is explicitly ecological and communal: it describes eating that minimizes ecological impact, supports regenerative agriculture, and is consistent with the Baliscan principle of living within the carrying capacity of the archipelago. Komida de tera is not a rigid category — it encompasses a spectrum from strict veganism to reduced-meat diets — but it provides a shared vocabulary and ethical framework that normalizes plant-based eating across the confederation.

The Green Protocol's institutionalization of the KDT diet has made plant-based eating the default in most communal settings. Communal dining halls (''komidas'') typically offer a KDT option as the standard, with meat-containing dishes available as alternatives rather than the other way around. This institutional inversion — plant-based as default, meat as optional — is one of the most distinctive features of Baliscan food culture and is frequently cited by visitors as the most immediately noticeable difference between Baliscan and other AMDC dietary norms.

==== Political associations ====
{{Main|List of platforms in Balisca}}
Vegetarianism in Balisca carries political associations that differ from those in many other countries. While plant-based eating is often associated with the fringe ecologist movement in some cultures, in Balisca it is broadly distributed across the political spectrum. The [[Ecological Action]] platform has historically been the strongest advocate for institutional ''komida di terra'', but the practice is also consistent with the [[Bastan!]] platform's emphasis on [[W:Democratic confederalism|democratic confederalism]] and ecological autonomy, the [[Unidad]] platform's bright-green environmentalism, and even [[Razón y Justicia|''Razón y Justicia'']]'s cultural conservatism — which frames indigenous plant-based traditions as part of Baliscan national heritage.

The [[Kadarist Platform]] and [[Revolutionary Front]] have historically been less enthusiastic about KDT, viewing it as a lifestyle concern that distracts from structural economic transformation, but neither opposes it. The [[Cosmopolitan Collective]] platform treats KDT as a matter of individual autonomy, but has taken an ambigious pos rejects its institutionalization, arguing that dietary choices should be personal rather than policy-driven.

=== Communal dining ===
{{Main|People's commune}}
[[File:People's_commune_kitchen.png|thumb|Food preparation in a [[W:People's commune#Commune life|communal kitchen]] in [[Macaio]] ({{Circa|1978}})]]
The [[komida|''komida'']] (communal dining hall/kitchen) system is the primary institutional vehicle for Baliscan vegetarianism. Every neighborhood in the confederation has at least one komida, and the KDT option is standard in all of them. The komida system evolved from the revolutionary period's [[W:People's commune#Commune life|communal kitchens]] and was formalized under the [[Green Protocol]] as a key institution of Baliscan social life, through [[W:Communal meal|communal meals]]. The midday meal at the ''komida'' is the primary meal for most Baliscans, and the plant-based default at komidas has a profound normalizing effect on dietary practice — even Baliscans who eat meat at home typically eat KDT at the komida.

The komida system also serves as a site of culinary innovation, where plant-based dishes are developed, tested, and disseminated across the confederation. Many of the most widely consumed vegetarian dishes in Balisca — including the Estuarian ''[[fesolada pura]]'', the Cerdanyan ''[[arroz de muntanya]]'', and the Pindoraman ''[[mokeka de kóko]]'' — originated in komida kitchens and spread through the informal network of communal dining halls.

== Health implications ==

Balisca has one of the lowest rates of [[W:Adult obesity|adult obesity]], a fact frequently attributed to the high prevalence of vegetarianism and the physically active lifestyle of the Baliscan population. The confederal public health system has historically emphasized the health benefits of KDT, and the Green Protocol's dietary guidelines recommend plant-based eating as a preventive measure against [[W:Cardiovascular disease|cardiovascular disease]] and certain cancers.

However, the health benefits of widespread vegetarianism are partially offset by other risk factors. Fifty percent of Baliscan adults self-report having at least one major risk factor for chronic disease: smoking, [[W:Physical inactivity|physical inactivity]], unhealthy eating, or excessive [[W:Alcohol use|alcohol use]]. Three chronic diseases — cancer (the leading cause of death), cardiovascular diseases, and respiratory diseases — account for 85 percent of all deaths in Balisca. Public health officials have noted that vegetarianism does not automatically equate to healthy eating: the high consumption of fried snacks (''[[patacón reyeno]]'', ''[[bolinho de mandioka]]'', ''[[pastel de koxinha]]''), refined carbohydrates, and sugary beverages (''[[atolé]]'', ''[[chicha de mora]]'') among Baliscan vegetarians contributes to metabolic risk factors independent of meat avoidance.

The [[Secretariat of Health and Care Services]] ''Protocolo Salú'' (Health Protocol), updated in 2023, recommended a shift in public health messaging from the binary of vegetarian versus omnivorous diets toward a more nuanced framework emphasizing whole foods, reduced processing, and balanced nutrition — regardless of whether the diet includes animal products. The Health Protocol acknowledged that the Green Protocol's emphasis on KDT had been effective in reducing meat consumption but had not adequately addressed the consumption of processed plant-based foods, fried snacks, and sugary drinks.

== Cuisine ==

{{Main|Baliscan cuisine}}

Baliscan vegetarian cuisine draws on four major culinary traditions: the indigenous [[Kurupiranga]] and [[Wallche people|Wallche]] plant-based heritage, the predominately [[W:Hinduism|Hindu]] and [[Buddhism|Buddhist]] vegetarian tradition, the [[Catharism|Catharist]] abstention from taking the lives of any living creature for consumption, and the South Kaftian legume-based cuisine of Araucaria. The fusion of these traditions with the broader Baliscan culinary landscape — Mesogean, Bayani, Darasian, Imfezzan — has produced a vegetarian cuisine of remarkable diversity and depth.

Key vegetarian dishes include ''[[mukúta de yerva]]'' (Araucarian vegetable communal stew with forest mushrooms), ''[[tajín de verdura]]'' ([[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan-Baliscan]] vegetable tagine), ''[[kari de pindóra]]'' (coconut vegetable curry), ''[[arroz de muntanya]]'' (Cerdanyan mountain rice with wild mushrooms and herbs), ''[[fesolada pura]]'' (Estuarian vegetarian bean stew), ''[[mokeka de kóko]]'' (coconut-vegetable stew), and ''[[sancoxo de yerva]]'' (plant-based stew). The standard vegetarian snacks — ''akara'', ''empanada de verdura'', ''kumú de guakamole'', ''bolinho de mandioka'', and ''kui de arroz'' — are widely available at market stalls and komidas.

Baliscan vegetarian desserts are often naturally plant-based, relying on coconut, cassava, tropical fruits, and nuts rather than dairy or eggs. The ''[[bolo de mandioka]]'' (cassava cake), ''kokada'' (coconut candy), ''[[sorvete de fruta]]'' (fruit sorbets), and ''pudín di kóko'' (coconut pudding) are among the most widely consumed desserts in the confederation and require no modification for vegan consumption.

